---
/**
 * foodcraft / HowBN — "Panday Coffee Works" HOW IT WORKS (design element
 * data-component="HOW-IT-WORKS", "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html" line 166).
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one procedural block — how an order is actually placed at a
 * roastery whose counter is a roller door. It renders EIGHTH in PageBN's order
 * (Header, Hero, Trust, About, Services, Why, Gallery, HOW, …), between the
 * gallery and the reviews, and it is THE ONE INK SECTION on the page: the
 * design paints its ground --ink and runs the spine straight through it. The
 * only other section this sheet does not draw on bone is the CTA band, which
 * the design paints rust. That inversion is the whole point of the
 * block — the procedure is the one thing the visitor is meant to stop and read —
 * and it is why almost every colour in this file is restated rather than
 * inherited: the wrapper's primitives are all tuned for a PAPER ground.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from the design element:
 *   section    border-bottom 1px #17181A, grid minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr),
 *              background #17181A                            → .sec + --ink
 *   rail       border-right 1px #33353A, flex-end/centre,
 *              padding 20px 0                                → .rail + --ink-3
 *   rail label rotated mono 10px / .3em caps, #E0713F        → .rail-t + --orange
 *   body cell  padding 52px 34px                             → .sec-body override
 *   h2         800, clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px)/1, -.03em, caps,
 *              margin 0 0 38px, #F2F1ED                      → .h2 + .hbn-h2
 *   grid       repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)),
 *              border-top + border-LEFT 1px #33353A          → .hbn-grid
 *   cell       padding 30px 26px,
 *              border-right + border-bottom 1px #33353A      → .hbn-cell
 *   badge      mono 10px / .14em caps, background #E0713F,
 *              colour #17181A, padding 4px 10px              → .hbn-n
 *   h3         700, 22px, -.02em, caps, margin 17px 0 9px,
 *              #F2F1ED                                       → .hbn-t
 *   body copy  15px / 1.78, #B4B6BA, margin 0                → .hbn-p
 *   action     margin-top 34px, btnOrange = the wrapper's own
 *              .btn base + background --orange / colour --ink
 *              / border --orange / padding 15px 28px         → .btn.btn--orange
 * Not one hex is written in this file (R1). btnOrange (design line 413) is
 * character-for-character the wrapper's `.btn.btn--orange`, so this file adds NO
 * button rule at all beyond the full-width wrapping label at 520px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group id "how" and group id "hero" — a data-field on any other path is a dead
 * hook that no sidebar input owns)
 *   how.headline             — fed with set:html so an owner can place this
 *                              design's second voice inside their OWN sentence.
 *                              Guarded: lib/derive-content-defaults.ts supplies
 *                              no how.headline, so an unset value must draw
 *                              nothing rather than open a 38px gap above the grid
 *   how.steps.${i}.title     — ORIGINAL index, see LIST INDEXES
 *   how.steps.${i}.body      — canonical key, spelled in the hook even when the
 *                              stored value arrived as .desc / .description, so
 *                              the first save heals the row onto the schema key
 *   hero.cta1.text           — the orange button's label
 *   hero.cta1.href           — its target. Deliberately the page's ONE primary
 *                              action reused rather than a new field: the schema
 *                              declares hero.cta1.text as a `link` FieldSpec
 *                              whose hrefPath is hero.cta1.href, so both halves
 *                              are owned by a real sidebar input. The design
 *                              reuses its own contactUrl/contactLabel in exactly
 *                              this slot
 *
 * DERIVED, not content:
 *   THE RAIL LABEL "05 / Procedure". The spine's index is decorative and derived
 *   per the wrapper contract — never an editable hook, never stored. 05 is this
 *   section's position among the railed sections, and it is the number the
 *   design itself prints here. It is aria-hidden: it is a string this FILE
 *   wrote, not the owner's, so it belongs to the drawing and not to the document
 *   outline.
 *
 *   THE STEP NUMERAL, and this is the decision a reader is most likely to think
 *   is an oversight. The design prints "Step 01" / "Step 02" / "Step 03" in the
 *   orange chip. The numerals are the designer's own zero-padded index and are
 *   reproduced exactly; THE WORD "Step" IS NOT, because it is a word of visible
 *   copy this component would be writing onto a business's page (R2). What the
 *   design SPECIFIES is the treatment — a filled orange chip, mono 10px at .14em
 *   caps, 4px 10px — and that is reproduced character for character. The chip is
 *   aria-hidden and carries no data-field, so it can never become a content key
 *   an owner has to maintain; the list is a real <ol>, so the ordinal reaches
 *   assistive tech through the markup instead.
 *
 *   THE TRACK COUNT. `--cols` follows the DATA, capped at the design's three.
 *   This grid is drawn with BORDERS — top and LEFT on the row, right and bottom
 *   on every cell — so hardcoding three tracks under two filled steps would leave
 *   the top hairline running on across a third of the band with no cell beneath
 *   it and the bottom rule stopping short. At three steps or more the computed
 *   value is character-for-character the design's repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)).
 *
 *   THE LIST ROLE. The <ol> carries role="list", because `list-style: none` is
 *   exactly what makes Safari/VoiceOver drop list semantics — and the ordinal
 *   reaching assistive tech through the markup is the entire reason the visible
 *   chip is aria-hidden.
 *
 * COLOUR ON AN INK GROUND — the three token choices worth reading:
 *   1. TEXT IS --on-ink, NOT --card. The design writes #F2F1ED for both the h2
 *      and the h3, and #F2F1ED is --card by value. --card is NOT used, because
 *      genericThemeOverrides.ts assigns --card from palette.card (an independent
 *      SURFACE value) while it computes --on-ink with pickOn(palette.ink) — i.e.
 *      --on-ink is derived FROM the very token painting this ground and stays
 *      paired with it under every scheme, and --card does not. On the native
 *      palette the two differ by at most 3/255 per channel (--on-ink #EFEEEA on
 *      --ink = 15.30:1; --card #F2F1ED would be 15.72:1), so the drawing is
 *      unchanged and the band cannot be repainted into illegibility.
 *   2. THE RAIL LABEL AND THE EMPHASIS ARE --orange, NOT --accent. The rail
 *      label is the DESIGN's own switch: every other rail on this sheet is
 *      #A8351B and this one is #E0713F (design line 168). The EMPHASIS has no
 *      design precedent either way — the design's own headline in this element
 *      carries no second voice at all — so its colour is this file's decision,
 *      and the arithmetic is what forces it: --accent #A8351B on --ink #17181A
 *      is 2.70:1, below AA at any size, while --orange #E0713F on --ink is
 *      5.59:1 — and --orange is the mark the design has already put on this
 *      band. The wrapper paints `:where(.h2 em)` --accent from its global
 *      block, so an owner's emphasis inside their own headline would land at
 *      2.70:1 on this one band unless this file overrides it — which it does,
 *      through :global() (R3), since a scoped rule cannot reach an element
 *      set:html created.
 *   3. THE STEP BODY HAS NO TOKEN. The design's #B4B6BA is the one colour in
 *      this element that no wrapper token holds; per R1 this file says so rather
 *      than inventing a hex, and composes the value from a token instead:
 *      rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .72) resolves over --ink to #B3B2B0, which is
 *      8.39:1 against the ground (the design's own #B4B6BA is 8.75:1). Composing
 *      it rather than freezing it is also the more robust choice — both ends of
 *      the mix are engine-owned, so the softened body colour keeps its
 *      relationship to the band under any scheme.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The design's
 * own steps — "Drinks are pulled to hit your minute", "Beans are ground to
 * order, never in advance", "Pay on collection, card or GCash fine" — are a
 * punctuality guarantee, a freshness claim and a list of accepted payment
 * methods made on behalf of a business nobody has spoken to, so none of them
 * ships. A shop that has said nothing about its process gets no process section
 * at all: blank rows are dropped and an all-blank list removes the whole
 * <section>.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5) = rows.length, the BODY content. It cannot count how.tag,
 * because derive-content-defaults hard-codes how.tag to 'How it works' on EVERY
 * site; it does not count how.headline either, for the same class of reason — a
 * gate on a headline ships a heading over an empty band. The CTA is not counted:
 * a section whose only content is a button is a heading over nothing. When the
 * gate fails the whole ink band goes, and the gallery above meets the reviews
 * below on their shared bone ground with the spine unbroken.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field carries the ORIGINAL array index; the orange
 * chip carries the DISPLAY index. Dropping a blank row 2 therefore renumbers the
 * visible chips 01/02 but cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2.
 *
 * ARRAY SOURCE. how.steps is canonical, and the hook ALWAYS spells how.steps —
 * the read-tolerant / write-canonical split every other How section in the repo
 * uses. A legacy site whose rows live under how.items still RENDERS, but its
 * hooks are not re-pointed at how.items: genericContentSchema declares no
 * how.items list and no fallbackPaths for it, so a hook spelled that way would
 * be inert in the sidebar.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - how.tag. THE DESIGN GIVES THIS SECTION NO EYEBROW — the h2 sits alone at
 *     the top of the cell, and the rail's "05 / Procedure" is this design's
 *     label slot. Rendering the eyebrow anyway would put a .kick above the
 *     headline that appears nowhere in the drawing, and derive-content-defaults
 *     ships how.tag on every site, so it would appear on every site. The field
 *     stays in the sidebar and is simply unused by this template.
 *   - No id on the <section>. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     visit / faq / main; the design gives HOW-IT-WORKS no id and no nav link
 *     points at it, so inventing #how would add a target nothing aims for.
 *   - No .body primitive on the step copy. Its colour, its size and its leading
 *     are all wrong on this ground — it is --ink-soft (a paper-ground colour),
 *     clamp(14px,1.3vw,15px) and line-height 1.62, against the design's flat
 *     15px / 1.78 on a light-on-dark mix. Opting in and then overriding every
 *     declaration would be more code, not less.
 *   - No .fig primitive on the numeral. .fig is --accent on paper; this numeral
 *     is --ink on a filled --orange chip.
 *   - No focus-ring override. The wrapper's ring is `outline: 3px solid
 *     var(--paper)` with an --accent halo outside it; the inner paper ring is
 *     15.30:1 against this ink ground, which is the strongest it is anywhere on
 *     the page, so the dark band needs no special case.
 *   - No `target="_blank"` on an IN-PAGE action. The design hard-codes one
 *     because its own href is an external contact URL, but the same button may
 *     now resolve to '#visit', and opening an in-page anchor in a new tab is a
 *     bug. Emitted for http(s) only.
 *   - No star row, no rating, no plate, no role="img": this section has none in
 *     the design and there is no upload behind it.
 *   - No max-width on the headline. The design states none on this h2; the
 *     wrapper's .h2 caps itself at 20ch, so the cap is removed here rather than
 *     silently halving the design's measure.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped <style> block, no is:global. The section class is
 * "hbn" and every scoped class is prefixed hbn- for THIS template: an audit had
 * to rename a set of classes copied verbatim from a sibling, so nothing here
 * reuses HowBM's hbm-* or HowBL's how-band / how-grid / how-step / how-num
 * names. Button classes are .btn / .btn--orange only — buildOverrideCss pins
 * label colours on .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .nav-cta / .cta-band .btn /
 * .testi .btn with !important, and none of those selectors reaches anything here
 * (R4). Astro 5 scopes by APPENDING [data-astro-cid-…], an attribute selector
 * worth (0,1,0), to this file's own class selectors — so the cheapest rule below
 * is `.hbn-h2[data-astro-cid-…]` at (0,2,0), and it is (0,2,0) that outruns the
 * wrapper's `html[data-page="BN"] :where(…)` primitives at (0,1,1). The scope
 * attribute on its own would not: it is the class it lands on that wins. No
 * !important appears anywhere in the file.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


const how = content.how || {};
// Trimmed, and otherwise untouched: this goes out through set:html, so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the em rule below intact.
const headline = typeof how.headline === 'string' ? how.headline.trim() : '';

type Step = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
// Read tolerantly — a legacy site stores its rows under how.items — and write
// canonically. See ARRAY SOURCE above.
const source: Step[] = Array.isArray(how.steps) ? how.steps : (Array.isArray(how.items) ? how.items : []);
const titleOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.body ?? s?.desc ?? s?.description ?? '').trim();
// A step the admin added but never filled would otherwise print an orange chip
// over nothing inside a fully ruled cell, and a title of pure whitespace would
// ship an empty <h3>. Normalise first, drop the blanks (whitespace counts as
// blank) and keep the ORIGINAL index on every survivor.
const rows = source
  .map((s, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(s), body: bodyOf(s) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// Track count follows the DATA, capped at the design's three — see THE TRACK
// COUNT above. Three or more filled steps compute exactly the design's
// repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)).
const cols = String(Math.min(Math.max(rows.length, 1), 3));

// Anchor guard (R7). Resolve in-page hrefs against the ids PageBN actually
// renders — menu, about, gallery, visit, faq, main — and map what
// derive-content-defaults emits onto them. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http)
// pass straight through untouched. Keep this table in step with the copies in
// the other BN sections.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};
const ctaText = typeof hero.cta1?.text === 'string' ? hero.cta1.text.trim() : '';
// #visit is where this template puts the address, the hours and the way to reach
// the shop, so it is the honest landing place for an action that cannot be
// resolved — the last step has just told the visitor where to collect.
const ctaHref = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#visit');
// Carried for http(s) ONLY — see NOT RENDERED above.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sec hbn">
    <div class="rail">
      {/* DERIVED chrome: the spine's index. No data-field, never stored. */}
      <span class="rail-t" aria-hidden="true">Procedure</span>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 hbn-h2 reveal" data-field="how.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}

      <ol class="hbn-grid" role="list" style={`--cols:${cols}`}>
        {rows.map((step, k) => (
          <li class="hbn-cell reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {/* DERIVED chrome: display index, aria-hidden, no data-field. The
                <ol> already carries the ordinal for assistive tech. */}
            <span class="hbn-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {step.title && <h3 class="hbn-t" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.title`}>{step.title}</h3>}
            {step.body && <p class="hbn-p" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.body`}>{step.body}</p>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      {ctaText && (
        <div class="hbn-act reveal">
          <a
            class="btn btn--orange"
            href={ctaHref}
            target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
            rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
            data-field="hero.cta1.text"
            data-href-field="hero.cta1.href"
          >{ctaText}</a>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* The spine (.sec / .rail / .rail-t / .sec-body), the display face, the caps,
     the tracking and both halves of the orange button all arrive from the
     wrapper's primitives. What this file owns is THE INVERSION: the ink ground,
     the hairline colour that goes with it, and every text colour that has to
     change because of it. Nothing else is restated.

     No literal end-of-style token appears anywhere in these comments. */

  /* THE ONE INVERTED SECTION. The design paints this section's ground --ink and
     leaves the spine's bottom rule (--rule-strong, the same value) in place, so
     the band's lower edge is drawn by the BONE section beneath it rather than by
     a visible line. `color` is set on the section as well as on every child: a
     colour that leaked in from the wrapper would be --ink text on an --ink
     ground. */
  .hbn {
    background-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--on-ink);
  }

  /* The rail's hairlines. The wrapper draws them in --line (#C8C7C2), which is a
     PAPER-ground hairline and would read as a bright stripe down a dark band;
     the design uses #33353A here, which is --ink-3. Both edges are set in one
     rule because the wrapper's shared 760 pass swaps border-right for
     border-bottom — above 760 the bottom border is 0-width and its colour is
     inert, so this needs no media query of its own. */
  .hbn .rail {
    border-right-color: var(--ink-3);
    border-bottom-color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  /* --orange, not --accent: 5.59:1 on this ground against --accent's 2.70:1.
     See COLOUR ON AN INK GROUND in the frontmatter. */
  .hbn .rail-t { color: var(--orange); }

  /* Design: padding 52px 34px — symmetric, where the wrapper's shared cell is
     44/34/40. Only the VERTICAL value is this section's; the horizontal clamp is
     the wrapper's own, character for character, so the ink band's text edge
     lines up with the bone sections above and below it. Any other value here
     would put a visible jog in the left margin of the sheet. */
  .hbn .sec-body {
    padding: clamp(34px, 4.6vw, 52px) clamp(20px, 3vw, 34px);
  }

  /* Design: clamp(28px,3.6vw,50px)/1, margin 0 0 38px, #F2F1ED, no measure cap.
     The wrapper's .h2 is clamp(28px,4.4vw,62px)/.98 in --ink at 20ch, and its
     shared 760 pass also carries `max-width:none` and `overflow-wrap:anywhere`.
     This rule outruns BOTH (see SCOPING), so those two safety declarations are
     restated here unconditionally rather than lost — they cost nothing at any
     width and they are what stops a long unbroken owner word at 360px. */
  .hbn-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.6vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0 0 38px;
    color: var(--on-ink);
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The headline's SECOND VOICE. The wrapper paints `:where(.h2 em)` --accent
     from its global block, which is 2.70:1 on this ground; --orange — the mark
     the design itself puts on this band — is 5.59:1. :global() because the headline
     is fed with set:html and a scoped rule cannot reach an element set:html
     created (R3). font-style is restated so the rule stands on its own if the
     .h2 class is ever dropped — Archivo has no italic worth using and the design
     never asks for one. */
  .hbn-h2 :global(em),
  .hbn-h2 :global(.em) {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--orange);
  }

  /* THE GRID IS DRAWN WITH BORDERS, NOT GAPS, and unlike the paper sections of
     this family it is FULLY BOXED: the design rules the top AND THE LEFT of the
     row, then the right and bottom of every cell, so the three steps read as a
     ruled table cut into the dark band. That left rule is drawn, not inherited —
     it is what separates a table on a dark ground from text floating on one.
     minmax(0,1fr) rather than 1fr so a long unbroken word in a step cannot widen
     its own track and push the page sideways. The inline custom property sets no
     grid-template-columns of its own, so the 900px rule below still wins on
     source order. */
  .hbn-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 3), minmax(0, 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink-3);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--ink-3);
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Design: padding 30px 26px, border-right + border-bottom.
     min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, so one unbroken word would still widen its track.
     LEADING IS RESET TO `normal`, which is the value the design's own cell
     inherits — its body declares none, while this repo's body declares 1.62.
     The h3 and the paragraph below both state their own line-height, so the
     only line this reaches is the chip's, where the wrapper's 1.62 strut would
     open leading under the chip that the design does not draw and push the
     title further from it than the design's 17px margin. */
  .hbn-cell {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 30px 26px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--ink-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink-3);
    list-style: none;
    line-height: normal;
  }

  /* THE FILLED CHIP. Design: mono, 10px, .14em caps, background #E0713F, colour
     #17181A, padding 4px 10px — the same orange/ink pairing the wrapper gives
     .btn--orange, deliberately taken from there so the chip and the button below
     it can never drift apart under a scheme. --ink on --orange is 5.59:1.
     display:inline-block because vertical padding on a pure INLINE box does not
     grow its line box, so the design's own `display` would let the filled chip
     ride up over whatever sits above it on a font whose metrics differ from the
     one the design was drawn in.
     NO line-height is stated, because the design states none: the chip inherits
     the `normal` reset on the cell above, which is what gives an inline-block
     the same drawn height as the design's inline background box. Pinning it to
     1 would make the chip SHORTER than the design draws it, since an
     inline-block's content height is its line-height rather than its font's
     content area. The design states no font-weight either, so none is stated
     here — it inherits the 400 the design inherits.
     No overflow-wrap: the content is a derived two-digit numeral. */
  .hbn-n {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    background-color: var(--orange);
    color: var(--ink);
    padding: 4px 10px;
  }

  /* Design: 700, 22px, -.02em, caps, margin 17px 0 9px, #F2F1ED. The face, the
     caps and the margin reset come from the wrapper's shared h1-h4 rule; the
     WEIGHT (800 there), the TRACKING (-.03em there), the LEADING (.98 there, but
     the design states none on this h3, so it computes to `normal`) and the
     COLOUR (--ink there, unreadable on this ground) are all this section's. */
  .hbn-t {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 22px;
    letter-spacing: -.02em;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 17px 0 9px;
    color: var(--on-ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: 15px / 1.78, #B4B6BA, margin 0. The .body primitive is not used —
     see NOT RENDERED. #B4B6BA has no token; rgba(var(--paper-rgb),.72) resolves
     over --ink to #B3B2B0 at 8.39:1, against the design's own 8.75:1. Qualified
     with the cell class so it beats the wrapper's bare-element paragraph reset
     (`:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}`). */
  .hbn-cell p.hbn-p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .72);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: margin-top 34px, left-aligned like everything on this sheet. */
  .hbn-act { margin-top: 34px; }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design, and they
     go straight to ONE at 900px rather than through a two-up: at 900 a 52px
     rail and 27px of body padding a side leave 793px for the tracks, so each of
     three is ~264px and 52px of cell padding leaves it ~210px of text, and
     three steps in two tracks leave a half-ruled orphan cell that reads as a
     broken table rather than an editorial one — which matters more here than on
     the paper sections, because this grid is boxed on all four sides.
     Stacked, the cell keeps BOTH vertical rules: the grid's left and the cell's
     right together close the box, which is the design's treatment for this band.
